  "data": {
    "text": "Stability of Whole Brain and Regional Network Topology\nwithin and between Resting and Cognitive States\nJustyna K. Rzucidlo, Paige L. Roseman, Paul J. Laurienti, Dale Dagenbach\nPLoS ONE 8(8): e70275 (2013). doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0070275\n\nAbstract\n\nNetwork topology was fairly stable across repeated sessions of the same task, but varied significantly between rest and working memory.\n\nEthics Statement\nThis research protocol was reviewed and approved by the Wake Forest University School of Medicine Institutional Review Board.\n\nParticipants\nData were collected from five young (23.6±1.67 years old) healthy adults who volunteered to partake in this study.\n\nImaging Study Design\nDuring each scan, fMRI data were acquired during five resting and five 2-back sessions, each lasting 4 minutes and 20 seconds. The resting sessions and working memory sessions were alternated. During the rest condition, a black fixation cross was presented on the screen in the goggles for the entire duration of the scan. In the task condition, the participants were presented with a sequence of letters, each two seconds apart. The participants had to respond with an answer of yes or no as to whether the letter was the same as the letter two before it.\n\nMR Image Acquisition\nAll imaging was performed on a Siemens SKYRA 3T MRI scanner. Whole-brain gradient echo echo-planar imaging (EPI) was used to detect BOLD fMRI signal changes. The echo planar imaging contained the following parameters: 120 volumes with 35 contiguous slices per volume; slice thickness 5.0 mm; in-plane resolution of 3.5 mm×3.5 mm; TR/TE=2000/25 ms.\n\nFunctional Image Pre-processing\nThe functional images were corrected for head motion by realigning them to the first image volume by using “rigid body” transforms. Next, the EPI image for each participant was co-registered, normalized to the standard stereotactic MNI space, and resliced to 4.0 mm×4.0 mm×5.0 mm. The images were not smoothed in order to prevent creating local false correlations. The time courses were extracted for each voxel in gray matter based on the Automated Anatomical Labeling (AAL) atlas and band-pass filtered to remove signals outside the range of 0.009–0.08 Hz. Mean global, white matter, and CSF signal as well as motion correction parameters were regressed from the filtered time series to account for physiological noise.\n\nNetwork Construction and Analysis\nA correlation matrix was produced by computing correlations between all possible pairs of voxels, then thresholded using the N=KS criterion to create binary adjacency matrices. Network properties including degree (K), global efficiency (Eglob), and local efficiency (Eloc) were computed. Whole-brain mean metrics were compared using 2 (condition) × 5 (session) repeated measures ANOVAs. ROI masks were defined for the precuneus and dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC).\n\nResults — Behavioral Performance\nParticipants’ accuracy on the 2-back task ranged from a mean of 86% to 95.4% across sessions; overall mean accuracy was 91.3% correct.\n\nThe functional images were corrected for head motion by realigning them to the first image volume by using ''rigid body'' transforms. Next, the EPI image for each participant was coregistered, normalized to the standard stereotactic MNI (Montreal Neurological Institute) space, and resliced to 4.0 mm 6 4.0 mm 6 5.0 mm. The images were not smoothed in order to prevent creating local false correlations [15].\n\nThe time courses were extracted for each voxel in gray matter based on the Automated Anatomical Labeling atlas [23] and band-pass filtered to remove signals outside the range of 0.0090.08 Hz [24,25]. Mean global, white matter, and CSF signal as well as motion correction parameters were regressed from the filtered time series to account for physiological noise. A correlation matrix was then produced by computing correlations between all possible pairs of voxels. The correlation matrices were thresholded using the following N = K S , where S is the equivalent of the shortest path length in a random network, N = number of nodes, and K=degree. The correlation coefficient that satisfied N = K S was used as a lower bound when creating binary adjacency matrices [25] This thresholding procedure ensures that the connection densities are consistent across conditions and individual subjects in the event that there is a change in the number of network nodes. Network properties were measured at various S values (2.5; 3.0; 3.5) and found to be independent of threshold effects (data not shown). The results presented here used S = 3.0 as a threshold."
  },
